"hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:"
2678,"**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ma'mar al-Qaysi, narrated to us Rawḥ, narrated to us Shibl, from Ibn Abi Najīḥ, from Mujāhid, who said: Abd al-Raḥmān ibn Abi Laylā narrated to me, from Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah (RA), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saw him while lice were falling on his face. He (ﷺ) said, ""Do these vermin trouble you?"" He replied, ""Yes."" So the Prophet (ﷺ) commanded him to shave his head while they were at al-Ḥudaybiyyah, though he had not made it clear to them that they could end their ihrām there, hoping they might enter Makkah. Then Allah, the Exalted, revealed the fidyah (compensation). So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded him to feed six poor people with one *faraq* (a measure of food), or offer a sheep as a sacrifice, or fast for three days.

Abu Bakr (RA) said: I have explained in my book on oaths and expiations the measurement of the *faraq*, that it is three *ṣāʿ* (a unit of measure), and that one *ṣāʿ* equals four *mudd* (a smaller measure), and that one *faraq* equals sixteen *ratl* (pounds), and that one *ṣāʿ* is one-third of it. Thus, the *faraq* is three *ṣāʿ*, and one *ṣāʿ* is five and one-third *ratl*, based on the evidences from the reports of the Prophet (ﷺ).

This verse is of the type in which Allah, the Exalted, has made a general commandment and then clarified its details through the tongue of His Prophet (ﷺ). For Allah, the Exalted, commanded in His Book regarding the fidyah for shaving the head: fasting, though He did not specify the number of days for fasting, nor the amount of charity, nor the number of poor to be fed, nor described the sacrificial rite. So the Prophet (ﷺ) clarified what Allah had obligated in the verse: that the fasting is for three days, the charity is three *ṣāʿ* to be distributed among six poor people, and that the sacrificial rite is a sheep.

The mention of the sacrificial rite in this report is of the type that indicates that the ruling by analogy (*qiyās*) and similarity (*shabah*) is obligatory. Thus, one-seventh of a cow or one-seventh of a camel is sufficient in the fidyah for shaving the head, or one-seventh of a cow or camel can take the place of a sheep in the fidyah, in the udhiyah (sacrificial offering), and in the hady (sacrificial animal for Hajj). The scholars do not differ that one-seventh of a camel or cow is sufficient in the hady of tamattuʿ, qirān, or udhiyah.

Some people of knowledge claim that one-tenth of a camel can take the place of a sheep in all of these cases. Thus, whoever permits one-tenth of a camel in these cases would permit one-seventh even more, since one-seventh is greater than one-tenth.

I have written a detailed discussion on this verse elsewhere, explaining that Allah, the Exalted, may obligate a thing in His Book by one meaning, while that same thing may be obligated by another meaning not mentioned in the Book, either through the tongue of His Prophet (ﷺ) or through the consensus of His Ummah. For Allah only obligated the fidyah in this verse for those who shaved their heads due to illness or harm, but the fidyah is also obligatory according to all the scholars for anyone who shaves his head in ihrām, even if there was no illness or harm, and even if he did so out of disobedience—provided he shaved his head while in ihrām.

I have explained in that discussion that the ruling by analogy (*qiyās*) and similarity (*shabah*) is obligatory in this case. If the ruling by analogy were not permissible, then the one who cuts his hair with scissors would not be required to pay any fidyah or expiation, since the term ""shaving"" (*ḥalq*) does not apply to cutting. But when the ruling by analogy is applied, then the one who cuts his hair in ihrām is required to pay the same fidyah as the one who shaves his head.

This is a lengthy discussion that I have elaborated elsewhere."
